---
import { PARTS, type PartId } from "@void-drift/mode-a";
import PartRows021 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows021.svelte";
import PartRows022 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows022.svelte";
import PartRows023 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows023.svelte";
import PartRows026 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows026.svelte";
import PartRows028 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows028.svelte";
import PartRows029 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows029.svelte";
import PartRows0210 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0210.svelte";
import PartRows0211 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0211.svelte";
import PartRows0212 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0212.svelte";
import PartRows0213 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0213.svelte";
import PartRows0214 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0214.svelte";
import PartRows0215 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0215.svelte";
import PartRows0216 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0216.svelte";
import PartRows0217 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0217.svelte";
import PartRows0218 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows0218.svelte";
import { PART_PROSE } from "../../../components/studies/part-prose";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const proseRows = (Object.keys(PART_PROSE) as PartId[]).map((id) => ({
  name: PARTS[id].name,
  prose: PART_PROSE[id],
  catalog: PARTS[id].description,
}));
const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "part-rows-2026-11-11");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A row that says whether the part is sanctioned</h2>
    <aside class="nb" aria-labelledby="nb">
      <h3 id="nb">Nota bene · the real goal, found in 02.3</h3>
      <p>
        The subject is the tool the player uses to choose the part for a slot.
        It is one bespoke thing in the Hangar. It has to do its own job well and
        conform enough to not feel off. It does not have to be a reusable
        pattern.
      </p>
      <p>
        The text below is the question as first asked. It leaves open what the
        row is made of without saying what the row belongs to, and the first
        three rounds answered it as a general list control.
        <a href="#tool">02.4</a>
        starts again from the tool.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      In <a href="/hangar">the Hangar</a> the player selects a mount, and a list
      shows one row for each part that mount accepts. Pressing a row fits its
      part. A part is either sanctioned for scored play or unsanctioned.
      Unsanctioned parts can be fitted only while Playground is on, when nothing
      is scored. The row has to say what the part is, whether it is fitted, and
      whether it is sanctioned. Today the fitted row is hard to tell from the
      row under the pointer, and an unsanctioned part carries only a small grey
      caption.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hard case is a forbidden part: an unsanctioned part that is still
      fitted when Playground is off. Turning Playground off unfits nothing, so
      the part stays on its mount and blocks launch. The fitted row is lime, the
      colour of a choice made. This row is fitted too, and it has to show that
      the choice must be undone.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Fixed</dt>
      <dd>
        Which parts a mount accepts, that pressing a row fits its part
        immediately, Playground's rules for launch and scoring, and what lime
        means.
      </dd>
      <dt>Hypothesis</dt>
      <dd>
        With Playground off, the list hides unsanctioned parts. A forbidden part
        stays in the list, and its row says the part must be removed, or
        replaced if the mount cannot be left empty. With Playground on,
        unsanctioned parts are listed and marked. This would change the
        Playground spec, which today shows them as unavailable.
      </dd>
      <dt>Open</dt>
      <dd>
        What the row is made of, how it shows each of the four states, and what
        words it uses for sanctioned and unsanctioned.
      </dd>
      <dt>Outside</dt>
      <dd>
        How the mount on the ship drawing shows the same states. The
        <a href="/lab/studies/ship-slots-2026-11-11">ship-slots study</a>
        covers that, and its answer must match this one.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <aside class="stamp" aria-labelledby="stamp">
      <h3 id="stamp">Question check · 11 November 2026</h3>
      <p>
        An independent reviewer tested this section against the question rubric
        and found no false claim. Of five findings, four are corrected above and
        one was declined. The section was then reworded for readability. The
        current text has not been retested.
        <a href="/lab/reviews/part-rows-2026-11-11">Full result and findings</a
        >.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Criterion</th>
            <th scope="col">Result</th>
            <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">One subject</th>
            <td>Pass with findings</td>
            <td>
              The study has one subject, the part row in four states. It also
              asks what the list contains and what the classes are called.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Grounded</th>
            <td>Pass with findings</td>
            <td>
              Every fixed item exists as described. The section left out how a
              fitted part is removed and what the Playground spec says today.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consequence</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              If the study succeeds, a row shows plainly whether its part is
              fitted and whether it is sanctioned, and a forbidden part looks
              blocked.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="settled">02 / Settled so far</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The subject</strong>
        is the tool for choosing the part for a slot: bespoke, amended and not
        reinvented (<a href="#anatomy">02.3</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The words</strong>
        are settled and held in the game spec (<a href="#terms">02.5</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The hatched band</strong>
        marks an unsanctioned part, in the colour of its component's border (<a
          href="#band"
          >02.6</a
        >).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A row keeps</strong>
        the name, a small drawing and one line of figures. Sentences of
        description do not belong in that line (<a href="#content">02.7</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The figures</strong>
        are a part's key game facts: one to four of them, each an icon and a
        number, always starting with mass, on one line at the bottom of the row
        (<a href="#density">02.8</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The card</strong>
        has a small drawing in its top left corner with little space around it,
        the name beside it, two lines of prose under the name, and the figures
        along the bottom. The prose says what the part is about and carries no
        figures (<a href="#card">02.10</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The line of figures</strong>
        has four columns, mass first. It stands 16 pixels from the card's left
        and right edges and 8 from what is above and below it. The figures are
        12 pixels (<a href="#prose">02.11</a>, <a href="#air">02.12</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>With Playground off,</strong>
        unsanctioned parts stay in the list, under the sanctioned ones, and
        cannot be fitted. The hypothesis in section 01, that they are hidden,
        did not hold: a part that vanishes when another is pressed looks wrong (<a
          href="#close"
          >02.17</a
        >).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The box</strong>
        has a button's cut shape and brackets and no fill of its own, on a pane
        at the first surface step. Fitted is a black ground with lit brackets. A
        part that has to come off has a faint red tint. An unavailable part is
        the whole card faded. The list opens with Keep empty (<a href="#empty"
          >02.18</a
        >).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The card is 300 pixels wide,</strong>
        always. The Hangar's other columns give way to it, the estimates first.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="pane">
      <summary>02.1 / The row as a pane of the console</summary>
      <p>
        The first answer builds the row the way the tabs are built. A row is a
        pane on the console surface with two cut corners and no outline. The
        drawing sits in a small dark window, so its colours hold in every state.
        The name leads, the catalog description follows in small text, and mass
        closes the row. The category letter is gone.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fitted</strong>
          fills the pane lime, as the selected tab does, and says Fitted. A
          pointer tints the other rows faintly and leaves the fitted row alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unsanctioned</strong>
          closes the row with a hatched band and replaces the description with
          “Unsanctioned · no score”. The band takes the row's own quiet colour,
          so it carries onto the lime of a fitted row.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Forbidden</strong>
          loses the fill and becomes a red frame. It keeps Fitted and the hatch,
          names the remedy, and cannot be pressed. The mount is emptied with
          Remove fitting, or another row is pressed.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The four lists follow the hypothesis: with Playground off, unsanctioned
        parts are hidden unless fitted. Rows can be pressed, and in the two
        lower lists the forbidden row leaves once its part is gone. Parts,
        classes, descriptions and drawings are the game's own. Each list keeps
        its own mount and fitting on this page only.
      </p>
      <PartRows021 client:load />
      <p>
        This shows how the rows look and behave in a list. It does not show them
        in the Hangar's column beside the ship, at the Hangar's width, or by
        touch. The words on the row are working words.
      </p>
      <h3>What 02.1 showed</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Kept: the hatched band.</strong>
          Diagonal lines in the padding at the end of the row mark an
          unsanctioned part. It reads as this game's own and it carries onto a
          fitted row. It is the working answer for the unsanctioned mark, not
          yet final.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stacked panes read as push buttons.</strong>
          The tabs work because they share one line whose two cut ends make it a
          single sliding control. Rows cut one by one and piled up lose that:
          each becomes a button, and a list of parts is not a set of buttons.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The dark window is wrong.</strong>
          A drawing boxed in black inside each row belongs to web games of the
          early 2000s, not to the 1980s games this one looks to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The row needs its container.</strong>
          Where the list sits, whether in a pane or in something that scrolls,
          is part of the answer and 02.1 did not give one.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="display">
      <summary>02.2 / One display, with the drawing outside the rows</summary>
      <p>
        The second answer stops building rows as controls. The list is one
        display set into a console pane, and a row is a line of it: a name, a
        short word at the end, a hairline beneath. Nothing is cut or raised per
        row. The display shows five lines and scrolls inside itself when the
        mount accepts more.
      </p>
      <p>
        The drawing leaves the row. A readout above the display shows one part
        at a time, larger and with no box around it: its drawing, name,
        description and figures. It shows the line under the pointer or keyboard
        focus, and otherwise the fitted part, so parts are compared by moving
        down the list before one is pressed.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fitted</strong>
          is the lit line: lime with dark text, ending in Fitted.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unsanctioned</strong>
          keeps the hatched band at the end of its line. The words “Unsanctioned
          · no score” are in the readout.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Forbidden</strong>
          is a red line with a red bar at its start, ending in Remove or
          Replace. It cannot be pressed; the readout names the remedy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <PartRows022 client:load />
      <p>
        The first list uses a universal mount so that it is long enough to
        scroll. The readout following the pointer does nothing on a touch
        screen, where a part's details would appear only once it is fitted. This
        still does not show the list beside the ship in the Hangar.
      </p>
      <h3>What 02.2 showed</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The row keeps its drawing, name and some figures.</strong>
          A readout outside the list does not work, because the list is never
          used alone. It is part of the Hangar, and the large picture of a part
          is already there: on the ship and its mount.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The drawing in the row is a small helper.</strong>
          It makes the row look like this game and lets ion be told from plasma
          by colour. It does not need to be large.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The open question is the minimum.</strong>
          What a row has to hold to work, and no more, has to be settled before
          its look.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="anatomy">
      <summary>02.3 / What a row holds, beside the ship</summary>

      <p>
        The third answer puts the list where it is used: beside the ship, in a
        slice of the Hangar. Choose a mount under the ship and the list shows
        its parts; fitting one changes the ship. The slice starts with
        Playground off and a rail gun on the first wing mount, so the forbidden
        row is the first thing shown.
      </p>
      <p>
        A row holds four things: a small drawing with no frame, the name, one
        line of figures, and a closing word only when the part is fitted. The
        fitted row alone adds the catalog description. The rows are lines of one
        list in the parts pane, which scrolls inside itself when the list is
        long.
      </p>
      <p>Two marks carry the states, and they do not share a place.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A bar at the start says fitted.</strong>
          Lime with the word Fitted when the part is permitted, red with Remove
          or Replace when it is forbidden. The row is not filled, so the drawing
          keeps its colours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The hatched band at the end says unsanctioned</strong>, as
          kept from 02.1. With Playground on, one line under the list explains
          it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <PartRows023 client:load />
      <p>
        The strip under the slice shows the row in each state at once. The
        slice's ship, mounts and fitting rules are the game's; its setting and
        fitting live only on this page. The slice is narrower than the Hangar
        and leaves out its other panes. It has not been tried by touch.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>What 02.3 showed</h3>
    <p>
      Rounds 02.1 to 02.3 each designed a row for a list, borrowing from the
      tabs and other shared controls. None of them looked as if it belonged in
      the Hangar. The reason is the subject, not the styling. The subject is the
      tool the player uses to choose the part for a slot. It is one bespoke
      thing in the Hangar. It has to do its own job well and conform enough to
      not feel off. It does not have to be a reusable pattern. Its nearest
      relative is the slot on the ship drawing, which it serves, and not the
      tabs. The work is to amend the tool that is in the Hangar, not to invent
      another. How it works is known, and so is where it sits: in its pane
      beside the ship. That pane predates the design system and needs a small
      change to join it. This is the main finding of the study so far.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="tool">
      <summary>02.4 / The tool as it stands, beside its slot</summary>

      <p>
        This round proposes nothing. It looks at the tool in the game today,
        with a slot selected, to see what it does and what it already shares
        with the slot. Below is the Hangar itself, live. It shows and changes
        the ship kept in this browser, and it will change when the Hangar does.
      </p>
      <div class="frame">
        <iframe src="/hangar" title="The Hangar, live" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <p>Carried in from the earlier rounds:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A hatched band at the end of the row marks an unsanctioned part. It is
          the working answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          The row keeps a small drawing, the name and some figures. The drawing
          is a helper for telling parts apart, and the large picture is on the
          ship.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>What the Hangar shows today:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The tool already names its slot.</strong>
          It is headed “Selected mount” with the slot's name, and one sentence
          says whether the slot can be left empty. It does not repeat the number
          the slot carries on the ship.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fitted is said the same way in both places.</strong>
          A filled slot on the ship is a thin lime rectangle around the part.
          The fitted row is a thin lime rectangle around the part. Whether by
          intent or not, the row already reads as the slot's contents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The same drawing appears twice.</strong>
          The part in the fitted row and the part in the slot are one drawing at
          two sizes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Selection belongs to the slot only.</strong>
          Lime corner brackets mark the slot being edited. Nothing in the tool
          is selected; its rows are fitted or not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The two disagree on words.</strong>
          The slot is labelled Engine, Equipment or Universal; a row is tagged
          E, G or U.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Neither says anything about class today</strong>
          beyond the small caption on the row. A forbidden part looks the same
          on the ship as a permitted one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A row is tall.</strong>
          Drawing, name, two lines of figures and a description make each row
          about a fifth of the pane's height, so two or three alternatives are
          visible at once, and the launch row covers the bottom of the list.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        What this suggests, to be tested and not yet shown: keep the tool and
        its pane, and amend the rows. They may take their language from the
        slot, with the fitted part as the slot's contents and the other rows as
        what could go in. Fitted, unsanctioned and forbidden would then be said
        once, the same way on the ship and in the tool.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="terms">
      <summary>02.5 / What things are called</summary>

      <p>
        Before the tool can be amended it has to be possible to talk about it.
        The game, its specs and its code use several words for the same thing,
        and one word for different things. This round collects them. The last
        column records what was settled in review on 11 November 2026. The game
        spec now holds them as its terms. The screens and the code have not been
        changed to match.
      </p>
      <div class="terms">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">The thing</th>
              <th scope="col">The game says today</th>
              <th scope="col">Specs, plan and code say</th>
              <th scope="col">After review, 11 November 2026</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">
                The place on a ship where a part goes
              </th>
              <td>
                Mount: “Selected mount”, “Fixed mounts”. Slot: “3 slots” on a
                ship tab, “Required propulsion slot”, “under-wing equipment
                slots”.
              </td>
              <td>
                Specs say mount about four times as often as slot. Code has
                both: the mount record, and the slot types and slot overlay. The
                accepted study is “Ship slots”.
              </td>
              <td>Settled: slot</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">What a slot accepts</th>
              <td>
                On the ship: Engines, Weapons, Support, Equipment, Universal,
                with the letters E, W, S, Q and +. In the pane's heading:
                “Engine”, “Equipment”, “Universal”, “mirrored E”, “Engine pair”,
                “Weapon / support”.
              </td>
              <td>Slot type: engine, weapon, support, equipment, universal.</td>
              <td>
                Settled: slot type. Engine, Weapon and Support each take one
                kind. Hardpoint takes a weapon or a support part; Equipment is
                dropped. Universal takes any part.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">The thing that is fitted</th>
              <td>
                Part: “Every installed part adds mass”. Also fitting: “Remove
                fitting”, “Static fitting”, “weapon/support fittings”.
              </td>
              <td>Part, throughout.</td>
              <td>Settled: part, or ship part in full</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">The kind of part</th>
              <td>A letter on the row: E, G or U. No words.</td>
              <td>
                Parts spec: engine, weapon, support. Code: categories E, G and
                U. The 0.6 plan lists the support parts under “Equipment”.
              </td>
              <td>Settled: engine, weapon, support</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">A part being in a slot</th>
              <td>
                “Fitted” on the row. “Installed” and “fittings” in the ship's
                description.
              </td>
              <td>Specs: fit, fitted, empty. Code: install, installed.</td>
              <td>Settled: fitted. A part is fitted to a slot, and removed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">Everything fitted to one ship</th>
              <td>
                “Setup”: “your active setup”, “this setup requires Playground”.
                “Build”: “Unsanctioned build”.
              </td>
              <td>
                Specs: fitting, and setup for the ship with its fitting. Code:
                loadout and build. The saved score has a field named fitting.
                The 0.6 plan marks “fitting” a placeholder.
              </td>
              <td>
                Settled: three words. The ship is the body, the fitting is the
                parts on it, and the build is the two together. Setup and
                loadout go. One part is never “a fitting”.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">The ship as a body</th>
              <td>
                Ship. Hull appears for the hit points, for “Hull base” speed and
                for “Hull 1” slots.
              </td>
              <td>
                Specs: ship and hull. Code and one Hangar spec line: chassis.
              </td>
              <td>Settled: ship. Chassis is unwanted in code too.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">The ship that will fly</th>
              <td>Not named on screen.</td>
              <td>Active ship, chosen 10 November 2026.</td>
              <td>Settled: active ship</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">The setting</th>
              <td>Playground, Off or On.</td>
              <td>
                Playground, chosen 11 November 2026. Code, the stored key and
                the spec's folder still say experimental.
              </td>
              <td>Settled: Playground</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">
                The two classes of ship and part
              </th>
              <td>
                “Unsanctioned” on a part, since today. “Unsanctioned build” on
                the title screen.
              </td>
              <td>
                Specs: stable and experimental, marked as placeholders; also
                eligible and excluded. Code: scored and preview. This study:
                sanctioned and unsanctioned.
              </td>
              <td>
                Sanctioned and unsanctioned, for the rules of ranked play only.
                See below.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">
                An unsanctioned part fitted with Playground off
              </th>
              <td>
                Title: “Unsanctioned build / Playground required”. Flight: “this
                setup requires Playground”.
              </td>
              <td>No word. This study's working word is forbidden.</td>
              <td>
                Settled: no new word. Not “forbidden”. The game's present
                wording for ranked play stands.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">Whether a run counts</th>
              <td>
                Scoring: “Scoring off”, “Top scores”, “Save score”. Ranking:
                “Survival is ranked”, “Unranked play”, “Unranked”.
              </td>
              <td>Specs use both: a run is scored, and ranked on the board.</td>
              <td>Settled: ranked, unranked</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="thing">Two slots that change together</th>
              <td>
                “Mirrored pair”, “Engine pair”, “linked”. “Linked engines” is
                also the name of a different setting.
              </td>
              <td>
                Mirrored pair, linked mount; Linked engines for the setting.
              </td>
              <td>
                Settled: linked slots, never mirrored. There may be more than
                two, and they always hold the same part.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Two reasons a part cannot be fitted</h3>
      <p>
        Sanctioned and unsanctioned say one thing: whether the rules of ranked
        play admit a ship or a part. A second reason is expected later and must
        not share those words. A player may not have the schematic for a part or
        a ship. A sanctioned part without its schematic still cannot be fitted
        for ranked play. The working words for that second axis are available
        and unavailable, to the player. Nothing in 0.6 builds it; the words are
        reserved so that the marks chosen here do not have to change.
      </p>
      <p>
        This bears on the tool now. The hatched band means unsanctioned for now.
        Whether it later also covers parts flown in Playground without their
        schematic, or those get another colour, is left for then. The Playground
        spec and the ship tabs use “unavailable” for an unsanctioned ship or
        part with Playground off, which is the word the second axis needs.
      </p>
      <p>Where one word means two things:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Equipment</strong>
          is a slot type that accepts weapons and support parts, and in the 0.6
          plan it is the support parts themselves.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fitting</strong>
          is one fitted part (“Remove fitting”) and everything fitted to a ship.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hull</strong>
          is the ship's hit points, the ship's body in the speed figures, and a
          group of slots.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Linked</strong>
          covers two things on purpose. Linked slots hold the same part: fitting
          one fits the others. Linked engines fire together. Both are
          connections between places on the ship, and both are to be drawn as
          lines on the ship drawing.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Smaller things seen on the way: part names are not cased alike (“Light
        ion”, “Light Ion Cannon”), and Practice is called test in code and in
        its spec's folder. Names of parts, ships and modes were not otherwise
        reviewed.
      </p>
