      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The layout inside,</strong>
          as settled: drawing in the corner, name, prose, figures along the
          bottom.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fitted:</strong>
          the brackets stay lit lime and a lime bar closes the bottom of the
          card. There is no word.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The hatched band,</strong>
          in the colour of the button's edge. A button's edge is its brackets,
          so the band is dim at rest and lights with them.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        An unsanctioned part still fitted with Playground off is the fitted card
        in red: brackets, bar and band.
      </p>
      <PartRows0215 client:load />
      <p>
        The lists and what a press does are the same as in 02.14. The cards
        stand on the page's black, where a button's pane is the second surface
        step.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>What 02.15 showed</h3>
    <p>
      A list of buttons does not work. When every card is a raised button the
      tool looks like a web game from the early 2000s, where everything was a
      button. Two parts of the button did hold up: the bracketed edge and the
      cut corner. The open question is whether a card can keep those without
      being a button.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="edge">
      <summary>02.16 / The button's edge without its body</summary>

      <p>
        The same cards as 02.15, with two changes. They stand on a pane at the
        first surface step, which is where they will be in the Hangar. And they
        have no fill of their own. The pane shows through each card, and only
        the cut shape and the brackets at its ends mark it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing else is different. A pointer or keyboard focus lights the
        brackets, and holding tints the card. Fitted keeps its lit brackets and
        lime bar. The hatched band takes the bracket colour.
      </p>
      <PartRows0216 client:load />
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>What 02.16 showed</h3>
    <p>
      The card without a fill, on its pane, with a cut shape and brackets, is
      close. Four things were wrong: the cards stood too near each other, the
      bar along the bottom of the fitted card looked like web design, the
      hatched band was too narrow, and the part that has to come off needed more
      than red lines.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="close">
      <summary>02.17 / Four corrections</summary>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The gap</strong>
          between cards is 8 pixels, as between buttons in a vertical group.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The fitted card</strong>
          stands on black, as the fitted row does in the Hangar today, with its
          brackets lit. No card has a bar along its bottom.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The hatched band</strong>
          is as wide as the card's corner cut, 10 pixels.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The part that has to come off</strong>
          has a faint red tint behind it, with red brackets and a red band.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <PartRows0217 client:load />
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>What 02.17 showed</h3>
    <p>
      The card is nearly there. Three things came out of using it. A slot needs
      a way to be emptied from the list itself. A change of state should not
      snap. And the hypothesis from section 01 failed: with Playground off,
      pressing another part made the unsanctioned one vanish from the list,
      which looks wrong. Unsanctioned parts have to stay listed and be
      unavailable, placed under the sanctioned ones.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="empty">02.18 / Keep empty, a fade, and parts that stay listed</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Keep empty.</strong>
        The list opens with a short card that holds the words Keep empty, in the
        type of a part's name, with no drawing. Pressing it empties the slot.
        When the slot is empty, this card is the fitted one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A fade.</strong>
        When a card becomes fitted or stops being fitted, its black ground and
        its brackets fade over the same short time the game's buttons use.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unsanctioned parts stay listed.</strong>
        They come after the sanctioned parts. With Playground off they cannot be
        pressed. The whole card is faded to a quarter of its strength, band
        included, and a pointer does nothing. Nothing in the card is turned
        grey.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <PartRows0218 client:load />
    <p>
      In the third list, press another part: the Rail gun stops being red and
      stays in the list, unavailable, beside the Laser. A slot that cannot be
      left empty, such as a required engine, would have no Keep empty card; that
      is not shown here.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>What 02.18 settled</h3>
    <p>
      The card in 02.18 is the result of the exploration. One more thing is
      fixed with it: the card is always 300 pixels wide. It does not stretch or
      shrink with the window. Where the Hangar is short of width, as on an iPad
      in landscape, the estimates column narrows; the card and the ship chooser
      keep their sizes.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="readiness">03 / What the Hangar has, and what it lacks</h2>
    <p>
      This part compares the card in 02.18 with the Hangar as it is built today,
      for delivery in 0.6.
    </p>
    <h3>Already there</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Which parts a slot accepts, fitting on a press, linked slots and
        required engines. None of this changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        The shared button rules. They give the card its cut shape, its brackets,
        what happens under a pointer, on focus and when held, and the timing of
        the fade.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each part's class, sanctioned or unsanctioned, and each part's drawing.
      </li>
      <li>The parts pane, which already stands at the first surface step.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Has to change</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong
          >The row is built inside the Hangar's one large component,</strong
        >
        markup and styles together. The card needs to be a component of its own.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two shared button rules do not suit the card:</strong>
        the pane behind a button, and the lime bar under a pressed button. The
        examples switch them off locally. The game needs a stated way to do
        that.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The figures.</strong>
        The Hangar writes most of them as fixed text, and one of those texts
        disagrees with the game's data. The examples compute them. That
        computing has to live with the game's rules, and Loading coil and
        Gravity coil need a figure in the data; today they have only mass.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The prose.</strong>
        The catalog's descriptions are long and some repeat the figures. Twelve
        short lines replace them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The order.</strong>
        The catalog mixes the classes. The list has to put sanctioned parts
        first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Emptying a slot.</strong>
        Keep empty replaces the Remove fitting button under the list. One
        browser test presses that button by name.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The caption.</strong>
        The word Unsanctioned on the row goes; the band says it. The same marker
        component is still used on a Lab page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The column.</strong>
        The Hangar's three columns are flexible: the ship column is at least 360
        pixels, the parts column at least 250, the estimates at least 270, with
        20 between. For a 300 pixel card the parts column has to be fixed at
        342. The three then need 1012 pixels at their narrowest. The page keeps
        36 pixels on each side, so a window 1024 wide, an iPad in landscape,
        leaves the columns 952. The estimates column has to give up at least 60
        pixels there, or fold away.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Missing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Icons.</strong>
        The seven icons in the examples are placeholders. The game has none for
        mass, thrust, energy, cruise ceiling, damage, reach or capacity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Approved words.</strong>
        The twelve lines of prose are drafts, and the game's tone of voice is
        not decided.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Still open</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A pointer and the fitted state both light the brackets. Only the black
        ground tells them apart.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keyboard and touch were not tried on the card. Whether an unavailable
        card can take keyboard focus is not decided.
      </li>
      <li>
        The slot on the ship has to show the same states. That is the
        <a href="/lab/studies/ship-slots-2026-11-11">ship-slots study</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The faded card means unsanctioned with Playground off. A part the player
        has no schematic for will need a look of its own later.
      </li>
      <li>Three parts have no drawing, so their corner is empty.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Nothing here blocks delivery in 0.6. The icons and the prose can ship as
      provisional and be replaced.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="scope">04 / The work, in slices</h2>
    <aside class="nb">
      <h3>Design principles</h3>
      <p>
        This study proposes no change to <code>docs/design.md</code>. The
        hatched band and the card belong in the design system's and the Hangar's
        specs.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      The reference for all of it is the example in
      <a href="#empty">02.18</a>
      at 300 pixels wide. Each slice can be delivered by itself, in this order.
    </p>
    <div class="terms">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Slice</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
            <th scope="col">Size and what it needs</th>
            <th scope="col">How to check it</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="thing">1 / The hatched band</th>
            <td>
              Write the rule into the design system's spec and give the shared
              styles one way to draw it: as wide as the corner cut, in the
              colour of the thing's edge.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">Small. Nothing first.</td>
            <td class="plain">
              The design system's Lab page shows the band on a dim, a lit and a
              red edge, and it follows the edge when that changes.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="thing">2 / Key figures</th>
            <td>
              Move the computing of a part's figures into the game's rules
              package. Give Loading coil and Gravity coil a figure in the data.
              Remove the fixed figure text from the Hangar.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              Small to medium. Nothing first. Changes the parts spec.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              A weapon's figures come from the same values its firing uses. No
              figure is written as text anywhere.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="thing">3 / Prose</th>
            <td>
              Replace the catalog's twelve descriptions with two-line prose that
              carries no figure.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              Small. Needs the words reviewed. Changes the parts spec, which
              today asks for a description that states what the part does.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              Every line fits two lines on a 300 pixel card. None contains a
              number.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="thing">4 / The card</th>
            <td>
              Build the card as its own Hangar component with its states:
              available, fitted, unsanctioned, has to come off, unavailable. Add
              Keep empty and remove the Remove fitting button. List sanctioned
              parts first. Remove the caption from the row.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              Medium to large. Needs slices 1 and 2; works with today's
              descriptions until 3. Changes the Hangar and Playground specs, and
              the design system's where the two button rules are concerned.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              Side by side with 02.18 at 300 wide, state by state. The Hangar's
              browser tests pass, with the one that presses Remove fitting
              rewritten for Keep empty. Keyboard and iPad touch are tried.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="thing">5 / The columns</th>
            <td>
              Fix the parts column so the card is 300 pixels wide at every
              window size. Let the estimates column narrow, and decide what it
              does on an iPad in landscape.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              Small to medium. Best with slice 4. Changes the Hangar spec.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              The card measures 300 at 1280 wide and at 1024 wide. Nothing in
              the Hangar scrolls sideways.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="thing">6 / Icons</th>
            <td>
              Draw the seven icons for the figures and replace the placeholders.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              Design work first, then small. Can follow the release; the
              placeholders stay until then.
            </td>
            <td class="plain">
              Each icon is told from the others at 10 pixels.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Not part of this work:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Changing the words on screen to the settled terms, such as mount to
        slot.
      </li>
      <li>How the slot on the ship shows these states.</li>
      <li>Schematics, and the look of a part the player does not have.</li>
      <li>The game's tone of voice.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This scope sizes the work. It does not start it; building it needs its own
      go-ahead.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
